# /etc/usb-hotplug/pools.conf — USB pool mappings
#
# Pools solve the "two identical USB devices" problem that standard PVE
# mappings can't: when you have N copies of the same VID:PID with no unique
# iSerial (game controllers, WiFi adapters, DACs, etc.), there's no way to
# tell them apart. A pool dynamically assigns whichever devices are present
# to a fixed range of consecutive usbN slots on a target VM, sorted by USB
# path (lowest path = first slot).
#
# Format — one pool per non-comment line, whitespace-separated:
#
#   <pool_name>  <vid:pid>  <target_vmid>  <first_slot>  <max_slots>  [usb3]
#
# Fields:
#   pool_name    free-form, used in logs only
#   vid:pid      4-hex:4-hex (lowercase), the USB VID:PID of the device class
#   target_vmid  the VM the devices should attach to (must be on this node)
#   first_slot   the lowest usbN slot index the pool owns (0..14)
#   max_slots    the upper bound on number of devices to attach simultaneously
#                — extras beyond this count are ignored
#   usb3         optional 6th field — pass "usb3" or "1" to force usb3=1 on
#                attach. Omit for default (USB2 emulation).
#
# Behavior:
#   - Pool fires every poll alongside the standard mapping logic.
#   - Slots in [first_slot, first_slot+max_slots-1] that contain a non-pool
#     entry (mapping=..., host=<vid:pid>, etc.) are LEFT ALONE. The pool only
#     touches slots whose value is host=<bus-port> (the format it writes).
#   - Pool entries targeting cross-node VMs are silently skipped.
#   - On hotplug: new device matching the VID:PID attaches to the next free
#     pool slot. On unplug: the corresponding slot is deleted from VM conf.
#
# Slot identity caveat: pool members are interchangeable. Slot N always
# attaches to whichever pool device has the lowest current USB path. If you
# unplug one and plug it into a different port, slots may shift on the next
# poll. For most use cases (controllers, wifi adapters) that's fine.

# Example: 2× SteelSeries Stratus Duo dongles (1038:1430) → VM 110 slots usb4 + usb5, USB3 enabled
# steelseries  1038:1430  110  4  2  usb3

# Example: 2× Alfa AWUS036NH USB wifi (148f:3070) — one on this host's VM 200 router
# alfa-router  148f:3070  200  6  1
